Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** example that shows how to:

1. **Add a button** to the page (you can style it any way you like).  
2. **Listen for the click** and call `map.flyTo()` with a set of options that give a smooth, “camera‑fly” animation.  
3. **Guard against race conditions** (e.g., the user clicks the button many times while the animation is still running).  
4. **Keep the map accessible** – move focus to the map canvas after the fly so keyboard users know the view has changed.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – smooth flyTo on button click</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    body { margin:0; font-family:system-ui; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; left:0; right:0; }
    #flyBtn {
      position: absolute;
      top: 1rem; left: 1rem;
      padding: .5rem 1rem;
      background: #fff;
      border: 1px solid #999;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
      font-size: 1rem;
      z-index: 10;               /* keep it above the map */
    }
    #flyBtn:focus { outline: 2px solid #0066ff; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>
<button id="flyBtn" aria-label="Fly to New York City">Fly to NYC</button>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map (replace the style URL with yours)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // demo style – replace with your own
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco (lon, lat)
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0,
    // optional: improve accessibility for screen‑readers
    attributionControl: false
  });

  // Add the default navigation controls (zoom, compass, etc.)
  map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Define the target camera position
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const target = {
    // New York City
    center: [-74.0060, 40.7128],   // lon, lat
    zoom: 13,                     // a comfortable city‑level zoom
    bearing: 0,                   // north‑up (you can set a rotation if you like)
    pitch: 45,                    // a slight 3‑D tilt – optional
    // Animation tuning – these are the defaults, but you can tweak them:
    duration: 2500,               // ms (2.5 s)
    curve: 1.42,                  // “flight curvature”; 1.0 = linear, >1 = more curved
    easing: t => t,               // linear easing – you can use d3‑ease or any easing fn
    essential: true               // true = respects prefers‑reduced‑motion
  };

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Helper to cancel a running fly if the user clicks again
  // -------------------------------------------------
  let isFlying = false;
  function flyToTarget() {
    if (isFlying) {
      // Abort the current animation – MapLibre will stop it automatically
      map.stop();   // stops any ongoing transition
    }
    isFlying = true;

    // The actual fly
    map.flyTo(target);

    // When the animation finishes (or is cancelled) reset the flag
    map.once('moveend', () => {
      isFlying = false;
      // Give keyboard focus back to the map canvas so screen‑reader users know the view changed
      map.getCanvas().focus();
    });
  }

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ Wire the button
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const btn = document.getElementById('flyBtn');
  btn.addEventListener('click', flyToTarget);

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣ Accessibility niceties
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Make the canvas focusable (it isn’t by default)
  map.getCanvas().setAttribute('tabindex', '0');

  // Optional: announce the new view to screen readers
  // (requires a live region somewhere on the page)
  const live = document.createElement('div');
  live.setAttribute('aria-live', 'polite');
  live.style.position = 'absolute';
  live.style.left = '-9999px';
  document.body.appendChild(live);

  map.on('moveend', () => {
    const [lon, lat] = map.getCenter().toArray().map(v => v.toFixed(4));
    live.textContent = `Map moved to ${lat}° north, ${lon}° east, zoom ${map.getZoom().toFixed(1)}`;
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## Why `flyTo` (and not `jumpTo`?)

| Method | Visual effect | Typical use |
|--------|---------------|-------------|
| `map.flyTo(options)` | Smooth, curved “flight” that eases in/out, respects `duration`, `curve`, `easing`, and `essential` (prefers‑reduced‑motion). | UI actions where you want the user to *see* the transition (e.g., “Go to my location”, “Show the selected city”). |
| `map.easeTo(options)` | Linear‑ish pan/zoom without the extra “arc” that `flyTo` adds. | When you want a quicker, less dramatic move (e.g., stepping through a time‑series). |
| `map.jumpTo(options)` | Instant change, no animation. | When you need to sync the view with a non‑visual event (e.g., resetting after a resize). |

---

## Tuning the animation

| Option | What it does | Typical range |
|--------|--------------|---------------|
| `duration` (ms) | Total time of the animation. | 800 – 3000 ms; longer for long‑distance flights. |
| `curve` | Controls the “flight path” curvature. `1.0` = straight line, `>1` = more arced. | 1.2 – 1.5 for a natural feel. |
| `easing` | Function `t → [0,1]` that maps linear time to eased progress. | `t => t` (linear) or `t => t*t` (ease‑in). |
| `essential` | If `true`, the animation will be **skipped** when the user has `prefers-reduced-motion` enabled. | Always set to `true` for accessibility. |
| `zoom`, `bearing`, `pitch` | Same as in a static style – they are interpolated together with the center. | Any valid values; keep pitch ≤ 60° for good performance on most devices. |

---

## Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| The map jumps instantly despite calling `flyTo`. | `essential: true` and the user has **prefers‑reduced‑motion** enabled. | Either respect the preference (recommended) or set `essential: false` if you must animate. |
| The animation feels “jumpy” when the button is clicked repeatedly. | Multiple `flyTo` calls overlapping. | Call `map.stop()` before a new `flyTo`, or guard with a flag (`isFlying`). |
| The button is not reachable by keyboard. | No focus style or the button is hidden behind the map canvas. | Ensure the button has a visible `:focus` style and a high `z-index`. |
| Labels disappear while the camera is moving. | The style’s label layers have `text-allow-overlap: false` and the camera zooms too fast, causing label collision. | Keep `duration` reasonable (≥ 1500 ms) or set `text-allow-overlap: true` on low‑importance label layers. |
| The map looks blurry after the fly. | The map’s `pixelRatio` is not matching the device’s DPR (e.g., on high‑DPI screens). | Use `map = new maplibregl.Map({pixelRatio: window.devicePixelRatio});` – MapLibre does this automatically in recent versions, but it’s good to be explicit if you override defaults. |

---

## Quick‑reference snippet (just the JS part)

If you already have a map instance and a button, the core logic is only a few lines:

```js
const target = {
  center: [-74.0060, 40.7128],
  zoom: 13,
  pitch: 45,
  bearing: 0,
  duration: 2500,
  curve: 1.42,
  essential: true   // respects prefers‑reduced‑motion
};

document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  map.stop();               // cancel any in‑flight animation
  map.flyTo(target);
});
```

---

### TL;DR

* Use `map.flyTo({center, zoom, bearing, pitch, duration, curve, easing, essential})`.  
* Call it from a button’s click handler, optionally `map.stop()` first to avoid overlapping flights.  
* Set `essential: true` for accessibility, and move focus back to the map canvas (or announce the new view) after the animation ends.  

That’s all you need for a smooth, user‑friendly camera transition in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!
